𝗖𝗔𝗨𝗧𝗜𝗢𝗡: 𝗧𝗜𝗠𝗘 𝗧𝗢 𝗥𝗘𝗗𝗨𝗖𝗘 𝗥𝗜𝗦𝗞

I started reducing risk today and opening some hedges.

I see several factors that probably cannot take the market down by themselves, but together, with one additional catalyst that I do not see yet, could produce a 2% to 7% market drawdown from here.

𝟭. 𝗠𝗶𝗱𝘁𝗲𝗿𝗺-𝗲𝗹𝗲𝗰𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻 𝘀𝗲𝗮𝘀𝗼𝗻𝗮𝗹𝗶𝘁𝘆. In the nine midterm-election years since 1990, the market first gained a median 4.2% from the end of July to its peak, followed by a median maximum drawdown of 9.9%. The $SPX has now followed almost exactly the same pattern, gaining around 4% since the end of July.

𝟮. 𝗝𝗮𝗽𝗮𝗻. Japanese bond yields are rising while the Bank of Japan is moving away from years of extremely easy monetary policy. This could strengthen the yen and pressure the enormous yen carry trade. We saw in 2024 how quickly that trade can unwind and create selling across global markets.

𝟯. 𝗩𝗼𝗹𝗮𝘁𝗶𝗹𝗶𝘁𝘆 𝗶𝘀 𝗯𝗲𝗴𝗶𝗻𝗻𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝘁𝗼 𝘄𝗮𝗸𝗲 𝘂𝗽. The $VIX is still relatively low, but $VVIX is rising. When the volatility of the $VIX begins moving before the $VIX itself, it can be an early warning that larger investors are starting to pay for protection.

𝟰. 𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝗲𝘀𝘁 𝗿𝗮𝘁𝗲𝘀. Interest rates remain elevated, putting pressure on consumers, corporate profits and market valuations. If rates continue rising, they could become another important source of pressure on the market.

𝗔𝗻𝗱 𝗺𝗼𝘀𝘁 𝗶𝗺𝗽𝗼𝗿𝘁𝗮𝗻𝘁𝗹𝘆:

𝟱. 𝗢𝗶𝗹. Oil is approaching $100 again, with no solution in sight. If oil remains this high or moves above $100, it could revive inflation, reduce the possibility of rate cuts and put additional pressure on consumers and corporate margins.

None of these factors guarantees a correction, and I still do not see the final catalyst. But the ingredients are now in place.

I am not turning aggressively bearish. I am simply reducing some risk and adding hedges while protection is still relatively cheap.

